<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Volume</title>

  <page>
  <p>
    <alert>Section 6.2</alert>
  </p>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that the region bounded by the graph <m>y=4-x^2</m>
        and the <m>x</m>-axis is rotated about the <m>x</m>-axis.
        Determine the cross-sectional area of a typical slice and
        then compute the volume of the resulting solid.
      </p>
      <image width="30%" margins="0% 70%">
        <shortdescription>The region between the parabola and the
        horizontal axis</shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="pf-10-volume-1">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/10-volume-1.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="10">
      <p>
        A typical cross section has radius <m>4-x^2</m> so that the
        cross sectional area is <m>A(x)=\pi(4-x^2)^2</m>.  This gives
        the volume element <m>dV=\pi(4-x^2)^2~dx</m> and so the total
        volume is
        <md>
          V = \int_{-2}^2 \pi(4-x^2)^2~dx = 107.23
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        One way to obtain a sphere is to spin a circle about one of
        its diameters.  Find the volume of a sphere of radius 2 by
        taking the top half of the circle of radius 2 centered at the
        origin and spinning it about the <m>x</m>-axis.  You may
        remember that the upper semicircle of radius 2 is the graph
        <m>y = \sqrt{4-x^2}</m>.
      </p>
      <image width="30%" margins="0% 70%">
        <shortdescription>The upper semicircle of radius
        2</shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="pf-10-volume-2">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/10-volume-2.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="10">
      <p>
        A typical cross section has radius <m>\sqrt{4-x^2}</m> so that the
        cross sectional area is <m>A(x)=\pi(4-x^2)</m>.  This gives
        the volume element <m>dV=\pi(4-x^2)~dx</m> and so the total
        volume is
        <md>
          V = \int_{-2}^2 \pi(4-x^2)~dx = \frac{32\pi}3
        </md>,
        which agrees with the formula <m>V=\frac43\pi r^3</m> for the
        volume of a sphere having radius <m>r</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  </page>
  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Now imagine that we drill a hole out the sphere where the hole
        is centered on a diameter of the sphere and has radius 1.  The
        solid is described by the rotating the region as shown below.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sketch a typical cross-section, determine its area, and then
        find the volume of the resulting solid.
      </p>
      <image width="30%" margins="0% 70%">
        <shortdescription>The area above the line y equals 1 and below
        the circle of radius 2</shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="pf-10-volume-3">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/10-volume-3.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="10">
      <p>
        A typical cross section is a larger circle of radius
        <m>\sqrt{4-x^2}</m> with a smaller circle of radius 1 removed.
        This gives the
        cross sectional area
        <md>
          A(x)=\pi(4-x^2)-\pi = \pi(3-x^2)
        </md>
        and
        the volume element <m>dV=\pi(3-x^2)~dx</m> and so the total
        volume is
        <md>
          V = \int_{-\sqrt{3}}^{\sqrt{3}} \pi(3-x^2)~dx = 21.77
        </md>,
        which agrees with the formula <m>V=\frac43\pi r^3</m> for the
        volume of a sphere having radius <m>r</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the region between the curve <m>x=4-(y-1)^2</m> and
        the vertical axis.  Find the volume of the solid that results
        by spinning this region about the vertical axis.
      </p>
      <image width="30%" margins="0% 70%">
        <shortdescription>The region between a parabola opening to the
        left and the vertical axis</shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="pf-10-volume-4">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/10-volume-4.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="10">
      <p>
        Now we want to slice the region with horizontal slices so that
        the cross sections are circles having radius <m>4-(y-1)^2</m>.
        This gives the volume element
        <md>
          dV = \pi\left(4-(y-1)^2\right)^2 =
          \pi\left(16-8(y-1)^2+(y-1)^4\right)
        </md>.
        Therefore
        <md>
          V = \int_{-1}^3 \pi\left(16-8(y-1)^2+(y-1)^4\right) =
          107.23
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose there is a water tank in the shape of a cone with the
        tip of the cone pointing downwards.  The tank is three meters
        high and the top has a radius of one meter.  If the tank
        currently holds one cubic meter of water, how deep is the
        water in the tank?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="10">
      <p>
        Draw a picture of the cone to see that it can be generated by
        spinning the line <m>y=3x</m> about the vertical axis.
        The cross sectional areas are
        <md>
          A(y)=\pi(y/3)^2=\frac{\pi}{9}y^2
        </md>.
        If <m>h</m> is the height to which the water is filled, then
        <md>
          V = \int_0^h \frac{\pi}9y^2 = \frac{\pi}{27}h^3 = 1
        </md>
        so
        <md>
          h=\left(\frac{27}{\pi}\right)^{1/3} \approx 2.05
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
